import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Media from '../components/Media.astro';
---
<Base title="Learn Mode (Planned)" description="A future NMSh proposal for learning Bash and zsh through guided practice. Not available yet; architecture, curriculum and isolation require approval.">
  <div class="container"><header class="page-hero"><span class="badge">Planned · Future Learn Mode</span><h1>Learn the shell.<br />Understand the command.</h1><p class="lead">Read a lesson, try it, then work out why it behaved that way. A proposed place to learn Bash and zsh through practice, with skills you can use in any ordinary terminal.</p><div class="actions"><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/raiseCatError/notMyShell/issues/398">Read the proposal · #398 <span>↗</span></a></div></header>
    <Media id="learn-concept" />
    <section class="section split"><div class="split-copy"><span class="eyebrow">An idea, not a shipped feature</span><h2>A guide beside you.<br />Room to try.</h2><p>NMSh already keeps input, output and context together. Learn Mode would use that space to teach Bash and zsh themselves, rather than a collection of NMSh shortcuts.</p><p>On wide terminals, a lesson guide could sit beside a practice area. Smaller terminals would use adaptive tabs or a toggle, keeping both reading and practice usable.</p></div><div><ol class="number-list"><li><div><strong>Understand the concept.</strong><p>Short explanations, clear objectives, layered hints and a searchable course-like reading experience.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Try it, then inspect.</strong><p>Practice with files, pipes, quoting and scripts. Feedback should explain the actual result, not reward memorizing one exact command.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Return with your progress.</strong><p>Versioned checkpoints, unfinished practice files and resumable learning, designed to stay local.</p></div></li></ol></div></section>
    <section class="section"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Skills for an ordinary shell.</h2><p>The initial teaching shells proposed are Bash and zsh. Their differences must be taught honestly; Fish is deferred. No course, command or practice terminal is available from this page.</p></div><div class="feature-row"><h3>Find your footing</h3><p>Terminals and shells. Paths and directories. Files, permissions and how to ask for help.</p><span class="eyebrow">Proposed foundations</span></div><div class="feature-row"><h3>Connect ideas</h3><p>Streams and pipes. Quoting, variables, exit status and inspecting mistakes instead of guessing.</p><span class="eyebrow">Proposed practice</span></div><div class="feature-row"><h3>Make something work</h3><p>Write and edit small shell scripts, debug them, and apply the concepts in practical challenges.</p><span class="eyebrow">Proposed scripting</span></div></section>
    <section class="section"><div class="split"><div class="split-copy"><span class="eyebrow">Safety is an architectural gate</span><h2>Practice should not put<br />your computer at risk.</h2><p>A temporary folder is not a secure sandbox. A real shell can reach other files, credentials, processes and the network. Isolation has to be enforced outside the lesson.</p></div><div class="prose"><h3>Verified isolated real shell</h3><p>Real Bash or zsh practice would require proven OS-enforced containment, a bounded practice filesystem, no host home or credential mounts, no network by default, resource limits and reliable cleanup.</p><h3>Explicitly labeled simulation</h3><p>If strong isolation cannot be verified on a platform, the proposal requires a clearly labeled simulator or disabled execution. It must never silently fall back to your ordinary host shell. Simulation must not claim full Bash/zsh compatibility.</p><p>Dangerous demonstrations remain simulated. A command blacklist or confirmation prompt is educational feedback, not the security boundary.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="section"><aside class="note"><strong>Still subject to validation and approval.</strong><p>Architecture, security feasibility, original curriculum and learner testing must be approved before implementation. There is no release date, version commitment, waitlist or installable Learn Mode. This page introduces the proposal; it does not implement it.</p></aside><a class="text-link" href="/roadmap/">See the wider roadmap <span>→</span></a></section>
  </div>
</Base>
